The cvc3 binary is built for linux/x86_64 by build.sh, which runs the build in a
Debian 8 (jessie) container.

Two things about that build are not obvious and are easy to get wrong.

1. The compiler must predate GCC 6.

   Every GCC >= 6 rejects CVC3 2.4.1 (std::hash<char*> called on a const char*).
   The patch that circulates for this -- "Fix gcc v6 compile failures", carried by
   nixpkgs among others -- redefines CDMap's default HashFcn and
   ExprValue::s_charHash, and the resulting binary is broken: it links, runs, and
   answers "This is CVC3 version 2.4.1", but rejects every SMT-LIB 2 input with

       *** Eval Error: Unknown command: [_SEQ, [_TYPEDEF, Bool, _BOOLEAN], ...]

   This was reproduced on GCC 15 / bison 3.8 and on GCC 8 / bison 3.3, with and
   without zchaff.  Building unpatched on GCC 4.9 is correct.

   So a build that has only been checked for compiling proves nothing.  Check it
   against a known-good CVC3 on real benchmarks.

2. zchaff must be deleted before configuring.

   src/sat/xchaff* is copyright Princeton and its licence forbids distribution
   ("FOR INTERNAL USE ONLY. NO DISTRIBUTION PLEASE"; internal, noncommercial,
   research use only).  It must not end up in a binary we ship.

   The prebuilt binaries on cs.nyu.edu -- including the linux64 ones -- do
   contain it, which is why they cannot be used here even though they are the
   right version.  They are still useful as an oracle when checking point 1.

   After a build:  strings -a cvc3 | grep -c CSolver   must print 0.

The binary is statically linked, so the container's glibc does not reach the
shipped artifact.
